/-
# Derived subgroups of locally normal groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 6.9)

Kourovka Notebook, Problem 6.9 (Yu. M. Gorchakov), marked solved in the 21st issue: is the derived
subgroup of any locally normal group decomposable into a product of at most countable subgroups
which commute elementwise?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Order.CompletePartialOrder
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.LocallyNormalSecondCentreQuotientKourovka1Problem9Wanted

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.LocallyNormalDerivedCountableFactorsKourovka6Problem9Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 6.9 (Yu. M. Gorchakov). UnsolvedMath record
UM-00700356 (`KOU-6.9`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

A group is locally normal when every finite subset lies in a finite normal subgroup
(`IsLocallyNormal` of the Kourovka 1.9 entry, reused here). "Decomposable into a product
of at most countable subgroups which commute elementwise" is read as in the resolving preprint: `G'`
(Mathlib's `commutator G`) is generated by a family `(H i)` of countable subgroups of `G'` with `[H
i, H j] = 1` for `i ≠ j`, an internal central product. No bound on the cardinality of `G` or of the
index type is imposed.
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.LocallyNormalSecondCentreQuotientKourovka1Problem9Wanted
  (IsLocallyNormal)

/-- Kourovka 6.9 (Yu. M. Gorchakov): the derived subgroup `G'` of a locally normal group `G` is
generated by a family `(H i)` of countable subgroups of `G'` such that `H i` and `H j` commute
elementwise for `i ≠ j`. -/
def locallyNormalDerivedCountableFactors : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G], IsLocallyNormal G →
    ∃ (ι : Type) (H : ι → Subgroup G), (∀ i, H i ≤ commutator G) ∧ (∀ i, Countable (H i)) ∧
      (∀ i j, i ≠ j → ∀ x ∈ H i, ∀ y ∈ H j, x * y = y * x) ∧ (⨆ i, H i) = commutator G

/--
Resolved true: the 21st issue marks 6.9 solved, "Yes, it is", citing Jayadevan's 2026 preprint (an
AI-generated solution confirmed by M. Dixon), which proves that for every group `G` in which every
finite subset lies in a finite normal subgroup, `G'` is generated by a family `(Hᵢ)` of countable
subgroups of `G'` with `[Hᵢ, Hⱼ] = 1` for `i ≠ j`, with no restriction on the cardinality of `G`.
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 6.9 (Yu. M. Gorchakov); resolution: A.
Jayadevan, Countable central factors in the derived subgroup of a locally normal group, preprint,
Zenodo, 2026 (AI-generated solution confirmed by M. Dixon), Kourovka Notebook 21st issue, Problem
6.9, marked solved: Yes, it is; preprint, Theorem 1.1: G' is generated by a family (H_i) of
countable subgroups of G' with [H_i, H_j] = 1 for i ≠ j, https://zenodo.org/records/22729504.
-/
public theorem_wanted locallyNormalDerivedCountableFactors_holds :
    locallyNormalDerivedCountableFactors

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.LocallyNormalDerivedCountableFactorsKourovka6Problem9Wanted
